WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.347
Put on your socks, then your shoes.
Now try it the other way round.

2
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:12.840
Same two steps, a different
order, and a very silly outcome.

3
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:18.866
Sami is getting dressed. The algorithm
has two steps: socks, then shoes.

4
00:00:19.086 --> 00:00:22.523
Follow it in that order, and
Sami is ready to go outside.

5
00:00:22.743 --> 00:00:26.504
Now swap the two steps round.
Shoes first, then socks.

6
00:00:26.724 --> 00:00:30.393
Oh no! The socks are on
the outside of the shoes.

7
00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:34.328
Same two steps. A different
order. A different outcome.

8
00:00:34.828 --> 00:00:39.426
A jam sandwich is an algorithm
too. It has four steps to follow.

9
00:00:39.646 --> 00:00:42.525
Bread on the plate. Then
butter on the bread.

10
00:00:42.745 --> 00:00:46.228
Then jam on the butter, and
the top slice on the jam.

11
00:00:46.448 --> 00:00:51.417
Every step has its place. Now put
the top slice on before the jam.

12
00:00:51.637 --> 00:00:55.956
The jam ends up on the outside of
the sandwich, and on your fingers.

13
00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:59.243
A mistake in an algorithm is called a bug.

14
00:00:59.463 --> 00:01:04.339
Read this one. Bread. Pour milk
on the bread. Jam. Top slice.

15
00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:08.460
Milk does not belong in a jam
sandwich. That step is the bug.

16
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.348
A bug can also be a right step
that sits in the wrong place.

17
00:01:12.568 --> 00:01:17.584
The top slice before the jam is one
of those. Finding the bug comes first.

18
00:01:18.084 --> 00:01:21.613
When you have found the bug,
the next job is to fix it.

19
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:25.131
Take the wrong step out,
and put the right step in.

20
00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:28.648
The milk step goes out.
The butter step goes in.

21
00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:32.304
Or take that early top slice
and move it to where it belongs.

22
00:01:32.524 --> 00:01:36.472
Then run the algorithm again
and check. A jam sandwich!

23
00:01:36.972 --> 00:01:40.455
You can change an algorithm on
purpose, to make something new.

24
00:01:40.675 --> 00:01:44.204
Swap the jam step for cheese,
and you get a cheese sandwich.

25
00:01:44.424 --> 00:01:47.675
Add a step at the end, cut it
in half, and you get two halves.

26
00:01:47.895 --> 00:01:51.657
The steps decide what you get.
What you get is the outcome.

27
00:01:51.877 --> 00:01:54.245
Change a step, and you change the outcome.

28
00:01:54.745 --> 00:01:58.460
Some algorithms keep you safe.
Then the order really matters.

29
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.628
Crossing the road has four steps,
and you can put them in order.

30
00:02:02.848 --> 00:02:07.445
Stop at the kerb. Look both ways.
Listen for cars. Then walk across.

31
00:02:07.665 --> 00:02:12.959
Walk first and you are already in
the road. Stop, look, listen, walk.

32
00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:16.710
The same steps in a different
order do a different thing.

33
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.692
A bug is a mistake in the
steps. Find it, and then fix it.

34
00:02:20.912 --> 00:02:24.070
Change one step, and
the outcome changes too.
